// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row match_array_elem_array_binder_shape_lost_run (tier 3 origin, observed `run 1`).
// An ARRAY-typed element binder in a slice pattern loses its ARRAY SHAPE and reads a STACK ADDRESS.
//
// `match m { [_, y] => { out = y[1]; } }` over `m: [[i64; 2]; 2]`: rustc 1.98.1 --edition 2024
// MEASURED — compiles, runs exit 0, prints got=4. TODAY logosc compiles this clean and prints a
// stack address (measured got=140732666136904 on base, 140734683204680 on armed — it is an address,
// so the digits differ per run and the ORACLE is the value 4, not the wrong number).
//
// ⚠ PRE-EXISTING AND REACHABLE ON THE SHIPPED BINARY, measured on the base binary of round
// 2026-09-17l and on its armed binary alike. The local-array carrier below compiles on both.
// That round widened EXPOSURE only: the struct-FIELD spelling (`match s.m { [_, y] => y[1] }`,
// hand program b02) was refused by an MLIR verifier error before it and reaches this defect after.
//
// ⚠ NOT THE SAME ROW AS `array_typed_field_binding_shape_lost`, and the difference was checked
// rather than assumed: that row is a STRUCT-PATTERN FIELD binder (`R { a: v, y }`) whose verdict is
// a REFUSAL (`'arith.addi' op operand #0 … got '!llvm.array<2 x i64>'`), and it still reproduces
// unchanged on this round's armed binary. This one is a SLICE-PATTERN ELEMENT binder whose verdict
// is a clean compile and a wrong VALUE. Different site, different observation, different oracle.
// FIX ORACLE = compiles AND prints got=4, exit 0.
package match_array_elem_array_binder_shape_lost_run;
extern fn printf(fmt: *const u8, ...) -> i32;
fn main() -> i32 {
    let m: [[i64; 2]; 2] = [[1i64, 2i64], [3i64, 4i64]];
    let mut out: i64 = 0i64;
    match m {
        [_, y] => { out = y[1]; }
    }
    unsafe { printf("got=%ld\n".as_ptr(), out); }
    if out != 4i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
